<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch9-page2-stability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Stability and the Phase Line</title>

  <p>
    On the previous page, we found the equilibria of an autonomous
    differential equation <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = f(p)</m>. As we mentioned, we
    can study the behavior of solutions without actually finding the general
    solution. On this page, we look at solutions that start near an
    equilibrium and determine whether they move toward it or away from it.
    This ultimately lets us understand the long-term behavior of solutions.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ode-unstable-example">
    <title>An unstable equilibrium</title>
    <p>
      Recall that <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p</m> has general solution
      <m>p(t) = Ce^t</m>, and its only equilibrium is <m>p = 0</m>. Here are
      several solutions again, with the equilibrium highlighted:
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ode-unstable">
    <caption>
      Solutions of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p</m>. Solutions that start near the
      equilibrium <m>p = 0</m> move away from it.
    </caption>
    <image width="65%">
      <shortdescription>Solutions of dp/dt = p moving away from the equilibrium p = 0</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          The equilibrium <m>p = 0</m> is drawn as a highlighted horizontal line.
          Solutions starting at <m>p(0) = 2</m>, <m>1</m>, and
          <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> increase away from it, and solutions starting at
          <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>-1</m>, and <m>-2</m> decrease away from it.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
          axis lines=middle, xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$p$},
          xmin=-0.05, xmax=1.6, ymin=-5.5, ymax=5.5,
          ytick={-2,-1,1,2}, xtick=\empty, samples=60, clip=false,
          every axis plot/.append style={thick},
          xlabel style={anchor=north east}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
          width=8cm, height=6.5cm,
        ]
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:1.0116] {2*exp(x)};
        \addplot[blue!65, domain=0:1.6000] {1*exp(x)};
        \addplot[blue!40, domain=0:1.6000] {0.5*exp(x)};
        \addplot[red!40, domain=0:1.6000] {-0.5*exp(x)};
        \addplot[red!65, domain=0:1.6000] {-1*exp(x)};
        \addplot[red, domain=0:1.0116] {-2*exp(x)};
        \addplot[orange!90!black, line width=2pt, domain=0:1.6] {0};
        \node[anchor=west, text=orange!90!black, font=\small, align=left] at (axis cs:1.6,0) {equilibrium\\$p = 0$};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    A solution that starts slightly above 0 grows, and a solution that starts
    slightly below 0 decreases. Either way, it moves away from the
    equilibrium, no matter how close to 0 it starts. We call the equilibrium
    <m>p = 0</m> <term>unstable</term>.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ode-stable-example">
    <title>A stable equilibrium</title>
    <p>
      Now consider <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = -p</m>. A quick check shows that
      <m>p(t) = Ce^{-t}</m> is the general solution, since
      <m>\frac{d}{dt}\left(Ce^{-t}\right) = -Ce^{-t}</m>. Again, the only
      equilibrium is <m>p = 0</m>.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ode-stable">
    <caption>
      Solutions of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = -p</m>. Solutions approach the
      equilibrium <m>p = 0</m>.
    </caption>
    <image width="65%">
      <shortdescription>Solutions of dp/dt = -p approaching the equilibrium p = 0</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          The equilibrium <m>p = 0</m> is drawn as a highlighted horizontal line.
          Solutions starting at <m>p(0) = 2</m>, <m>1</m>, and
          <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> decrease toward it, and solutions starting at
          <m>-\frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>-1</m>, and <m>-2</m> increase toward it.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        \begin{axis}[
          axis lines=middle, xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$p$},
          xmin=-0.05, xmax=3, ymin=-2.5, ymax=2.5,
          ytick={-2,-1,1,2}, xtick=\empty, samples=60, clip=false,
          every axis plot/.append style={thick},
          xlabel style={anchor=north east}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
          width=8cm, height=6.5cm,
        ]
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:3] {2*exp(-x)};
        \addplot[blue!65, domain=0:3] {1*exp(-x)};
        \addplot[blue!40, domain=0:3] {0.5*exp(-x)};
        \addplot[red!40, domain=0:3] {-0.5*exp(-x)};
        \addplot[red!65, domain=0:3] {-1*exp(-x)};
        \addplot[red, domain=0:3] {-2*exp(-x)};
        \addplot[orange!90!black, line width=2pt, domain=0:3] {0};
        \node[anchor=west, text=orange!90!black, font=\small, align=left] at (axis cs:3,0) {equilibrium\\$p = 0$};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    This time, every solution approaches 0 as <m>t \to \infty</m>, from
    above or from below. We call the equilibrium <m>p = 0</m>
    <term>stable</term>.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ode-phase-line">
    <title>The phase line</title>
    <p>
      We will skip over some of the underlying theory, but the ideas for
      doing this with any <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = f(p)</m> are simple, as long as
      <m>f</m> is nice enough (for example, if <m>f</m> has a continuous
      derivative, which is true for every <m>f</m> in this class). In that
      case, solutions can't cross each other. In particular, a solution can
      never cross an equilibrium, so a solution that starts between two
      equilibria stays between them.
    </p>
    <p>
      This means we only need to know the <em>sign</em> of <m>f(p)</m>:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Where <m>f(p) \gt 0</m>, we have <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \gt 0</m>, so
            <m>p</m> is increasing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Where <m>f(p) \lt 0</m>, we have <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \lt 0</m>, so
            <m>p</m> is decreasing.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, take <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = (p - 2)(p + 3)</m> from the
      previous page, with equilibria <m>p = -3</m> and <m>p = 2</m>.
      Checking the sign of each factor:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            For <m>p \lt -3</m>, both factors are negative, so
            <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \gt 0</m> and <m>p</m> is increasing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For <m>-3 \lt p \lt 2</m>, <m>p - 2 \lt 0</m> and
            <m>p + 3 \gt 0</m>, so <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \lt 0</m> and <m>p</m> is
            decreasing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For <m>p \gt 2</m>, both factors are positive, so
            <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \gt 0</m> and <m>p</m> is increasing.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      We record this on a <term>phase line</term>: a number line for
      <m>p</m>, with a dot at each equilibrium and an arrow on each interval
      between them. An arrow pointing <alert>right</alert> means <m>p</m> is
      increasing there, and an arrow pointing <alert>left</alert> means
      <m>p</m> is decreasing.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ode-phase-line">
    <caption>
      Left: the graph of <m>f(p) = (p - 2)(p + 3)</m>, with the phase line
      below it. Right: sketches of solutions <m>p(t)</m>, with the equilibria
      highlighted. Arrows pointing right on the phase line correspond to
      solutions going up on the right.
    </caption>
    <image width="100%">
      <shortdescription>Graph of f(p) = (p - 2)(p + 3) with its phase line, and sketches of solutions</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          On the left is the graph of <m>f(p) = (p - 2)(p + 3)</m>, an upward
          parabola crossing the <m>p</m>-axis at <m>-3</m> and <m>2</m>. It
          is above the axis for <m>p \lt -3</m> and <m>p \gt 2</m>, labeled
          <q>dp/dt &gt; 0, p increasing,</q> and below the axis for
          <m>-3 \lt p \lt 2</m>, labeled <q>dp/dt &lt; 0, p decreasing.</q>
          Below the graph is the phase line: an arrow pointing right for
          <m>p \lt -3</m>, an arrow pointing left for <m>-3 \lt p \lt 2</m>,
          and an arrow pointing right for <m>p \gt 2</m>. The equilibrium
          <m>-3</m> is labeled stable and <m>2</m> is labeled unstable.
          On the right are solutions <m>p(t)</m>, with the equilibria
          <m>p = 2</m> and <m>p = -3</m> drawn as highlighted horizontal lines.
          Solutions starting above 2 increase without bound, solutions
          starting between <m>-3</m> and 2 decrease toward <m>-3</m>, and
          solutions starting below <m>-3</m> increase toward <m>-3</m>.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        % ---------- left: graph of f(p) with phase line ----------
        \begin{axis}[
          name=fplot,
          axis lines=middle, xlabel={$p$}, ylabel={$\frac{dp}{dt} = f(p)$},
          xmin=-5, xmax=4, ymin=-8, ymax=10,
          xtick={-3,2}, ytick=\empty, clip=false,
          xlabel style={anchor=north west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
          width=7.2cm, height=6.5cm, domain=-5:4, samples=80,
        ]
        \addplot[black, thick, domain=-4.53:3.53] {(x-2)*(x+3)};
        \node[text=blue, font=\scriptsize, align=center] at (axis cs:-4.1,6.5) {$dp/dt > 0$\\$p$ increasing};
        \node[text=red!80!black, font=\scriptsize, align=center] at (axis cs:-0.5,-3.2) {$dp/dt &lt; 0$\\$p$ decreasing};
        \node[text=blue, font=\scriptsize, align=center] at (axis cs:3.0,6.5) {$dp/dt > 0$\\$p$ increasing};
        % phase line below
        \draw[thick] (axis cs:-5,-11) -- (axis cs:4,-11);
        \draw[-latex, very thick, blue] (axis cs:-4.8,-11) -- (axis cs:-3.3,-11);
        \draw[-latex, very thick, red!80!black] (axis cs:1.7,-11) -- (axis cs:-2.7,-11);
        \draw[-latex, very thick, blue] (axis cs:2.3,-11) -- (axis cs:3.9,-11);
        \fill[black] (axis cs:-3,-11) circle (2.5pt);
        \fill[black] (axis cs:2,-11) circle (2.5pt);
        \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (axis cs:-3,-11.4) {$-3$ (stable)};
        \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (axis cs:2,-11.4) {$2$ (unstable)};
        \node[anchor=east, font=\scriptsize] at (axis cs:-5,-11) {phase line};
        \end{axis}
        % ---------- right: solutions p(t) ----------
        \begin{axis}[
          at={(fplot.east)}, anchor=west, xshift=1.6cm,
          axis lines=middle, xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$p$},
          xmin=-0.02, xmax=1.2, ymin=-5, ymax=4.5,
          xtick=\empty, ytick={-3,2}, clip=false,
          xlabel style={anchor=north east}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
          width=6.2cm, height=6.5cm,
          every axis plot/.append style={thick},
        ]
        \addplot[orange!90!black, line width=2pt, domain=0:1.2] {2};
        \addplot[orange!90!black, line width=2pt, domain=0:1.2] {-3};
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:0.3008, samples=80] {(2+3*(0.074074)*exp(5*x))/(1-(0.074074)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[blue!60, domain=0:0.5666, samples=80] {(2+3*(0.019608)*exp(5*x))/(1-(0.019608)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[red!80!black, domain=0:1.2000, samples=80] {(2+3*(-0.020408)*exp(5*x))/(1-(-0.020408)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[red!65, domain=0:1.2000, samples=80] {(2+3*(-0.250000)*exp(5*x))/(1-(-0.250000)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[red!50, domain=0:1.2000, samples=80] {(2+3*(-1.500000)*exp(5*x))/(1-(-1.500000)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[red!35, domain=0:1.2000, samples=80] {(2+3*(-9.000000)*exp(5*x))/(1-(-9.000000)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:1.2000, samples=80] {(2+3*(4.333333)*exp(5*x))/(1-(4.333333)*exp(5*x))};
        \addplot[blue!60, domain=0:1.2000, samples=80] {(2+3*(9.333333)*exp(5*x))/(1-(9.333333)*exp(5*x))};
        \node[text=blue, font=\scriptsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.55,3.6) {increasing};
        \node[text=red!80!black, font=\scriptsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.75,1.0) {decreasing};
        \node[text=blue, font=\scriptsize, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.55,-4.4) {increasing};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Understanding how the three pictures relate lets you visualize how the
    sign of <m>f(p)</m> determines what happens to the solutions. However, we
    can determine stability directly from the phase line:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          At <m>p = -3</m>, the arrows on both sides point <em>toward</em> the
          equilibrium. Solutions that start near <m>-3</m> approach it, so
          <m>p = -3</m> is <alert>stable</alert>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          At <m>p = 2</m>, the arrows on both sides point <em>away</em> from
          the equilibrium. Solutions that start near 2 move away from it, so
          <m>p = 2</m> is <alert>unstable</alert>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    You should be able to connect these ideas to the direction of the arrows
    and to the sketches of solutions approaching or moving away from the
    equilibria. However, the precise reason this always works depends on an
    existence and uniqueness theorem, which we won't go into.
  </p>


  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ode-semistable">
    <title>A semistable equilibrium</title>
    <p>
      There is one more possibility: the arrows on both sides can point the
      same way. For example, <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = (p + 1)^2</m> has the
      equilibrium <m>p = -1</m>, and <m>(p + 1)^2 \gt 0</m> on both sides of
      it.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ode-semistable">
    <caption>
      The phase line and solutions of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = (p + 1)^2</m>.
    </caption>
    <image width="90%">
      <shortdescription>Phase line and solutions of dp/dt = (p + 1) squared</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          On the left is the phase line, with the equilibrium <m>-1</m> labeled
          semistable and arrows pointing right on both sides of it. On the
          right are solutions <m>p(t)</m>, with the equilibrium <m>p = -1</m>
          drawn as a highlighted horizontal line. Solutions starting below
          <m>-1</m> increase toward it, and solutions starting above
          <m>-1</m> increase away from it without bound.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
        % ---------- left: phase line ----------
        \begin{scope}[yshift=2.2cm]
        \draw[thick] (-2.6,0) -- (2.6,0);
        \draw[-latex, very thick, blue] (-2.5,0) -- (-0.35,0);
        \draw[-latex, very thick, blue] (0.25,0) -- (2.5,0);
        \fill[black] (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
        \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-0.15) {$-1$ (semistable)};
        \node[above, font=\scriptsize] at (0,0.6) {phase line for $dp/dt = (p+1)^2$};
        \node[right, font=\scriptsize] at (2.6,0) {$p$};
        \end{scope}
        % ---------- right: solutions ----------
        \begin{axis}[
          at={(4.6cm,0)}, anchor=south west,
          axis lines=left, xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$p$}, ylabel style={rotate=-90},
          xmin=0, xmax=4, ymin=-3.2, ymax=2,
          xtick=\empty, ytick={-1}, clip=true,
          width=6.2cm, height=6cm,
          every axis plot/.append style={thick},
        ]
        \addplot[orange!90!black, line width=2pt, domain=0:4] {-1};
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:4, samples=80] {-1 - 1/(x + 0.500000)};
        \addplot[blue!65, domain=0:4, samples=80] {-1 - 1/(x + 1.000000)};
        \addplot[blue!40, domain=0:4, samples=80] {-1 - 1/(x + 2.500000)};
        \addplot[blue!40, domain=0:2.1667, samples=80] {-1 - 1/(x + -2.500000)};
        \addplot[blue!65, domain=0:0.9167, samples=80] {-1 - 1/(x + -1.250000)};
        \addplot[blue, domain=0:0.3333, samples=80] {-1 - 1/(x + -0.666667)};
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Solutions that start below <m>-1</m> approach it, but solutions that
    start above <m>-1</m> move away from it. We call <m>p = -1</m>
    <term>semistable</term>. Since some nearby solutions move away, a
    semistable equilibrium is not stable.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ode-technical-defs">
    <title>The technical definitions</title>
    <p>
      For all the equations in this class, stability will work out the way
      we have described, by reading the phase line. For reference, here are
      the technical definitions from Section 9.4.3 of the book, written for
      one variable.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <definition xml:id="def-ode-stability">
    <title>Stable and Asymptotically Stable</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>k</m> be an equilibrium of <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = f(p)</m>.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k</m> is <term>stable</term> if solutions that start near
              <m>k</m> stay near <m>k</m> in future time. More precisely, for
              each <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m> there is a <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such
              that if <m>|p(0) - k| \lt \delta</m>, then
              <m>|p(t) - k| \lt \epsilon</m> for all <m>t \geq 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>k</m> is <term>asymptotically stable</term> if solutions that
              start near <m>k</m> stay near <m>k</m> in future time, and in
              addition approach <m>k</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>. More
              precisely, for each <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m> there is a
              <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that if <m>|p(0) - k| \lt \delta</m>,
              then (i) <m>|p(t) - k| \lt \epsilon</m> for all
              <m>t \geq 0</m>, and (ii) <m>p(t) \to k</m> as
              <m>t \to \infty</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        An equilibrium that is not stable is called <term>unstable</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    On a phase line, if the arrows on both sides point toward an equilibrium,
    it is asymptotically stable. If the arrows on at least one side point
    away, it is unstable. So in this class, when we call an equilibrium on a
    phase line <q>stable,</q> we mean asymptotically stable.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-basin-of-attraction">
    <title>Basin of Attraction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>basin of attraction</term> of an asymptotically stable
        equilibrium <m>k</m> is the set of starting values <m>p(0)</m> whose
        solutions approach <m>k</m> as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    For example, for <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = (p - 2)(p + 3)</m>, every solution
    that starts below 2 approaches <m>-3</m>, so the basin of attraction of
    <m>p = -3</m> is <m>(-\infty, 2)</m>. Solutions that start at exactly
    <m>p(0) = 2</m> stay at 2, and solutions that start above 2 increase
    without bound.
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's practice with an equation that will look familiar in Chapter 10.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ode-phase-line">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which is the phase line for <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p(1 - p)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\longleftarrow \underset{0}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{1}{\bullet} \longleftarrow</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. For <m>p \lt 0</m>, <m>p \lt 0</m> and <m>1 - p \gt 0</m>,
            so <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \lt 0</m>. For <m>0 \lt p \lt 1</m>, both
            factors are positive, so <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \gt 0</m>. For
            <m>p \gt 1</m>, <m>1 - p \lt 0</m>, so <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \lt 0</m>.
            So <m>p = 0</m> is unstable and <m>p = 1</m> is stable.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\longrightarrow \underset{0}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{1}{\bullet} \longrightarrow</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. The arrows are backward. Try a value between 0 and 1,
            like <m>p = \frac{1}{2}</m>: both factors are positive, so
            <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \gt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\longrightarrow \underset{0}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{1}{\bullet} \longrightarrow</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Check the sign for <m>p \gt 1</m>: then
            <m>1 - p \lt 0</m>, so <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \lt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\longleftarrow \underset{0}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{1}{\bullet} \longleftarrow</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Check the sign for <m>0 \lt p \lt 1</m>: both factors
            are positive, so <m>\frac{dp}{dt} \gt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ode-basin">
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>\frac{dp}{dt} = p(1 - p)</m>, what is the basin of attraction
        of the stable equilibrium <m>p = 1</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>[0, \infty)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close, but check <m>p(0) = 0</m>: that solution stays at the
            equilibrium 0 forever, so it never approaches 1.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p><m>(0, \infty)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right. Every solution that starts above 0 approaches 1: from
            below if <m>0 \lt p(0) \lt 1</m>, and from above if
            <m>p(0) \gt 1</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>(0, 1)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Solutions that start above 1 also approach 1, since
            the arrow for <m>p \gt 1</m> points left.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>(-\infty, \infty)</m></p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Solutions that start below 0 decrease, moving away
            from 0 and never reaching 1.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-9-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=9.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
